#---
# purpose: "a binary segment's size can be a variable from the enclosing scope, not only one the pattern binds"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# # binary_variable_size
#
#     case bin do
#       <<s :: binary-size(n), rest :: binary>> -> {:ok, s, rest}
#       _ -> :error
#     end
#
#     take("hello world", 5)
#       Elixir  {:ok, "hello", " world"}
#       fz      :error          on every door, silently
#
# A LITERAL size worked on the same input, and so did a size bound by an EARLIER
# FIELD of the same bitstring. What did not work was a size from the enclosing
# scope -- a function parameter, or anything bound before the `case`.
#
# The dispatch plan resolved a size name against the bindings the PATTERN
# itself makes, and nothing else, so an outer name came back absent and the
# clause failed with no error and no warning. Plain destructuring was fine,
# because it goes through a different lowering that reads the environment.
#
# THE FIX IS THAT THIS IS THE SAME QUESTION AS PINNING. `^x` in a pattern is a
# name the pattern USES but does not BIND, which is exactly what an outer-scope
# size is, and pins already had the whole mechanism: a `PinnedValueId`, a pass
# that binds one to its parameter index, and a resolver on every door. A size
# name the pattern does not bind now becomes a pin, and
# `BitstringFieldSize::BindingName` -- which no door could resolve -- is gone.
#
# WHY IT WAS P1: without it no library function can take a slice of a binary
# whose length it computed, so every string-building routine has to accumulate
# one byte at a time.
defp take(bin, n) do
  case bin do
    <<s :: binary-size(n), rest :: binary>> -> {:ok, s, rest}
    _ -> :error
  end
end

defp head_tail(<<s :: binary-size(3), rest :: binary>>), do: {:ok, s, rest}
defp head_tail(_b), do: :error

defp inner(<<n, s :: binary-size(n), rest :: binary>>), do: {:ok, s, rest}
defp inner(_b), do: :error

def main() do
  dbg(take("hello world", 5))
  dbg(take("hi", 5))
  dbg(head_tail("abcdef"))
  dbg(inner(<<3, 97, 98, 99, 100>>))
end
